  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1016 PM MDT Mon Jun 8 2026\n\nPacific upper trough over the Great Basin and sub-tropical high over \nthe Gulf of America/east Texas continue to clash over New Mexico. \nThe high is able to hold its ground and allow south winds to \ntransport some moisture northward over our area. While this flow is \nnot a jackpot of moisture, it will keep our dewpoints in the 30s to \n40 Tuesday, enough for at least some enhanced cumulus/virga/light \nshowers. Will keep mentionable POPs limited to the mountains Tuesday \nfor now, along with below threshold POPs for the central lowlands. \n\nBuilding sub-tropical high will dominate the area into the weekend. \nFor Tuesday through Thursday the Pacific trough finally gives up and \nrides up over the north edge of the high and allow the heat to build \nup. Much of the lowlands can expect triple digit highs, and the \nusual hot areas (El Paso and Las Cruces metro, lower valley, and \nwestern Tularosa Basin) may need heat advisories by Wednesday and \nThursday. \n\nBy Friday into the weekend the upper high drifts back east some, \nover east Texas and the Gulf Coast. This will allow southerly flow \nover New Mexico and sub-tropical moisture to make some headway into \nthe area. Tropical features over far southwestern Mexican coast may \nalso contribute some to this flow. GFS shows PWs increasing to well \nover an inch (as high as 1.5\" but probably overdone a bit). So \ncombination of cloud cover and showers should knock high temps back \ninto the 90s. Of more concern would be the flood potential if we see \nthose PWs approaching 1.5\". ECMWF shows the same basic pattern, \nthough the moisture doesn't seem as robust. Will watch this \nsituation closely.",
